  "text": "The nemeton was a sacred forest clearing where the Celts held outdoor rites. Celtic worship took place mostly outside, in groves and open ground, rather than inside a building. Trees carried deep religious meaning in Celtic life, never more than in these clearings. The name links to older European roots: Greek nemos means glade, and Latin nemus means sacred. It may also grow from an old Irish root, nem, whose meaning covered heaven and all things holy. Strabo wrote of the oak groves where the Galatian clans gathered to worship. The poet Lucan gave a dark, shadowed image of such groves, where old god figures sat as rough blocks of carved wood. Fire and earth-motion might strike a sacred grove, yet the trees stayed whole. After Rome moved in, the word came to name stone temples and whole towns such as Medionemeton in Scotland.",
